#   synthetic_train.jsonl and synthetic_heldout.jsonl. The Hugging Face cache of #549, offline, no token.
#
# TEST ONLY: the scorer is rehearsal_scorer:team5, lm_eval_lab:team5 with the export's probe-loss compare
#   skipped. A #608 commit saves its probe loss (NLL summed over validation batch 0) under the device class it
#   trained on. On the GPU slot that is cuda:<name>, the export runs on cpu, so Team 7's export_safetensors
#   finds no cpu probe and skips the compare (ckpt.py: ok = saved is None or bits == saved). This run trains
#   on cpu, so the compare runs, and it fails because lm_eval_lab's probe is another quantity (mean loss on
#   random ids). The wrapper returns the saved cpu bits: the same skip as on the slot. Everything else (file
#   sha256, strict keys, scoring, records, the watcher) is the real code. Its full source:
#     """TEST ONLY, never for a lab job: lm_eval_lab:team5 with the export's probe-loss compare skipped.
#
#     Why: a #608 trainer saves its probe loss (NLL summed over validation batch 0) under the device class it trained on.
#     On the GPU eval slot the trainer's class is cuda:<name> and the export runs on cpu, so Team 7's export_safetensors
#     finds no cpu probe and skips the compare (ckpt.py: ``ok = saved is None or bits == saved``). A laptop rehearsal
#     trains on cpu, so the compare runs and fails, because lm_eval_lab's probe is a different quantity. This wrapper
#     returns the saved cpu bits, which is the same as the skip on the slot. Everything else (file sha256, strict keys,
#     scoring, records) is the real lm_eval_lab:team5 path.
#
#         run_lab_ckpt.py --scorer rehearsal_scorer:team5 <the same arguments as lm_eval_lab:team5>
#     """
#     import struct
#
#     import lm_eval_lab
#
#
#     class RehearsalScorer:
#         def __init__(self, inner):
#             self.inner = inner
#             self.name = inner.name
#             self.saved = None
#
#         def check(self, manifest):
#             self.inner.check(manifest)
#             bits = (manifest.get("probe_loss") or {}).get("cpu")
#             self.saved = struct.unpack(">d", bytes.fromhex(bits))[0] if bits else None
#
#         def factory(self):
#             return self.inner.factory()
#
#         def probe(self, model):
#             return self.saved if self.saved is not None else self.inner.probe(model)
#
#         def score(self, model, device):
#             return self.inner.score(model, device)
#
#         def __getattr__(self, name):  # anything else run_lab_ckpt.py reads from the scorer
#             return getattr(self.inner, name)
#
#
#     def team5(argv):
#         return RehearsalScorer(lm_eval_lab.team5(argv))
#
# Differences from the job (dry run 2 on the eval slot):
#   scorer lm_eval_lab:team5         -> rehearsal_scorer:team5                         (the probe compare above)
#   GPU, bf16                        -> CPU, fp32                                      (laptop)
#   Tier B --every 10                -> --every 3                                      (9 commits; it still skips step 0)
#   full item sets, --interval 60    -> --limit 2, --interval 5                        (time)
#   rows loaded into ClickHouse      -> --no-load (the same parse, no write)           (local only)
#   dry run 2 shape, KAS head        -> arms/t1.yaml (AdamW), --smoke, trainer on 1 thread (Muon's step-0 state takes too long on CPU; the KAS head is checked separately)
#   SIGKILL                          -> TerminateProcess (Popen.kill)                  (Windows; no handler runs in either)
# Scores: --limit 2 items per task (Tier A: 122 freeze-set items) on a model a few steps from random, so they
#   test the path, not the step-0 bands of eval_schedule.md section 5.2 (those need the full item sets).
# Edits to this log: progress-bar lines are left out; the laptop folders are shown as <rehearsal>, <lab>,
#   <c3>, <tmp>, <home>, the venv interpreter as python, the host name as <host>.
